window.CHECKLIST = {"sections":[{"key":"PHYS","label":"Physics"},{"key":"CHEM","label":"General Chemistry"},{"key":"BIO","label":"Biology & Biochem"},{"key":"ORG","label":"Organic Chemistry"},{"key":"LAB","label":"Lab Techniques"}],"cards":[{"code":"CPAS-01","cat":"PHYS","title":"Kinematics: throwing a ball straight up","locked":true},{"code":"CPAS-02","cat":"PHYS","title":"Forces, work, energy and power","locked":true},{"code":"CPAS-03","cat":"PHYS","title":"Fluids: pressure, buoyancy and flow","locked":true},{"code":"CPAS-04","cat":"PHYS","title":"Electrostatics: charges, fields and potential","locked":true},{"code":"CPAS-12","cat":"PHYS","title":"Circuits: current, resistance and meters","locked":true},{"code":"CPAS-13","cat":"PHYS","title":"Capacitors and dielectrics","locked":true},{"code":"CPAS-05","cat":"PHYS","title":"Optics: lenses, mirrors and light","locked":true},{"code":"CPAS-11","cat":"PHYS","title":"Waves, sound and the Doppler effect","locked":true},{"code":"CPAS-06","cat":"CHEM","title":"Gas laws: the basics","locked":true},{"code":"CPAS-07","cat":"CHEM","title":"Thermodynamics: ΔG, ΔH, ΔS and heat","hook":"Three formulas and their signs. That's the whole card.","formulas":[{"tex":"\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S","say":"Gibbs free energy"},{"tex":"\\Delta G^\\circ = -RT\\ln K_{\\text{eq}}","say":"free energy and the equilibrium constant"},{"tex":"q = mc\\,\\Delta T","say":"heat needed to change temperature"}],"vars":[{"tex":"\\Delta G","name":"change in Gibbs free energy","unit":"kJ/mol","eli5":"< 0 spontaneous, > 0 nonspontaneous, = 0 at equilibrium"},{"tex":"\\Delta H","name":"change in enthalpy","unit":"kJ/mol","eli5":"heat absorbed or released at constant pressure; < 0 exothermic, > 0 endothermic"},{"tex":"\\Delta S","name":"change in entropy","unit":"J/(mol·K)","eli5":"change in disorder (number of ways to arrange things)"},{"tex":"T","name":"temperature","unit":"K","eli5":"always Kelvin (capital $T$)"},{"tex":"R","name":"gas constant","unit":"8.314 J/(mol·K)","eli5":""},{"tex":"K_{\\text{eq}}","name":"equilibrium constant","unit":"no unit","eli5":"products ÷ reactants at equilibrium; always positive"},{"tex":"q","name":"heat transferred","unit":"J","eli5":"+ absorbed, − released"},{"tex":"m","name":"mass","unit":"g","eli5":""},{"tex":"c","name":"specific heat capacity","unit":"J/(g·K)","eli5":"water = 4.18 J/(g·K) = 1 cal/(g·°C)"},{"tex":"\\Delta T","name":"change in temperature","unit":"K or °C","eli5":"$T_{\text{final}} - T_{\text{initial}}$; the same number in K or °C"}],"eli5":"$\\Delta G$ is the reaction's \"want to.\" Negative $\\Delta G$ = the reaction rolls downhill on its own. Two things help it roll: releasing heat ($\\Delta H < 0$) and making things messier ($\\Delta S > 0$). Temperature decides how much the mess counts.","points":[{"t":"Signs of $\\Delta G$:","sub":["$\\Delta G < 0$ → spontaneous in the forward direction.","$\\Delta G > 0$ → nonspontaneous; needs energy input (the reverse is spontaneous).","$\\Delta G = 0$ → at equilibrium."]},{"t":"Signs of $\\Delta H$ and $\\Delta S$:","sub":["$\\Delta H < 0$ exothermic (combustion); $\\Delta H > 0$ endothermic (melting ice).","$\\Delta S > 0$: solid → liquid → gas, more moles of gas, dissolving. $\\Delta S < 0$: freezing, condensing, fewer gas moles."]},{"t":"$K_{\\text{eq}}$ and $\\Delta G^\\circ$:","sub":["$K_{\\text{eq}} > 1$ → $\\ln K > 0$ → $\\Delta G^\\circ < 0$: products favored.","$K_{\\text{eq}} < 1$ → $\\ln K < 0$ → $\\Delta G^\\circ > 0$: reactants favored.","$K_{\\text{eq}} = 1$ → $\\ln K = 0$ → $\\Delta G^\\circ = 0$."]},"During a phase change, heat goes in but $T$ stays flat, so use $q = mL$ (latent heat), not $mc\\Delta T$.","Catalysts lower the activation energy; they change the **rate**, never $\\Delta G$ or $K_{\\text{eq}}$."],"traps":["$\\Delta H$ is usually in **kJ**, $\\Delta S$ in **J**/K. Convert before plugging into $\\Delta H - T\\Delta S$.","Spontaneous does **not** mean fast. Diamond → graphite is spontaneous but takes forever.","$K_{\\text{eq}}$ is never negative. \"Large vs small\" (above or below 1) is what matters."],"mnemonics":["$q = mc\\Delta T$ reads **\"m-c-AT\" = MCAT**.","$\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S$: **\"Go Home (minus) Tired Student.\"**","**\"Big K, negative G.\"**","Temperature is the tie-breaker: **high $T$ lets entropy win; low $T$ lets enthalpy win.**"],"fig":"cpas07-quadrants.svg","figcap":"The four sign combinations of ΔH and ΔS, and when each reaction is spontaneous."},{"code":"CPAS-08","cat":"CHEM","title":"pH, pKa and buffers","locked":true},{"code":"CPAS-09","cat":"CHEM","title":"Titrations","locked":true},{"code":"CPAS-10","cat":"CHEM","title":"Electrochemistry: galvanic vs electrolytic cells","locked":true},{"code":"BBFL-01","cat":"BIO","title":"Enzyme kinetics: the Michaelis-Menten equation and Km","hook":"Km is the most tested number in biochemistry. Know what it is and what it is not.","formulas":[{"tex":"v_0 = \\dfrac{V_{\\max}[S]}{K_m + [S]}","say":"Michaelis-Menten equation"},{"tex":"V_{\\max} = k_{\\text{cat}}[E]_T","say":"maximum rate depends on how much enzyme you have"},{"tex":"\\text{catalytic efficiency} = \\dfrac{k_{\\text{cat}}}{K_m}","say":"the best single measure of a good enzyme"},{"tex":"K_m = \\dfrac{k_{-1} + k_{\\text{cat}}}{k_1}","say":"what Km is made of"}],"vars":[{"tex":"v_0","name":"initial reaction rate","unit":"M/s","eli5":"speed at the start, before product builds up"},{"tex":"V_{\\max}","name":"maximum rate","unit":"M/s","eli5":"speed when every enzyme is busy (saturated)"},{"tex":"[S]","name":"substrate concentration","unit":"M","eli5":"how much \"food\" the enzyme has"},{"tex":"K_m","name":"Michaelis constant","unit":"M","eli5":"the $[S]$ that gives half of $V_{\\max}$"},{"tex":"k_{\\text{cat}}","name":"turnover number","unit":"s⁻¹","eli5":"reactions one enzyme finishes per second when saturated"},{"tex":"[E]_T","name":"total enzyme concentration","unit":"M","eli5":"all enzyme present: free enzyme $[E]$ + enzyme holding substrate $[ES]$"},{"tex":"k_1,\\ k_{-1}","name":"binding and unbinding rate constants","unit":"M⁻¹s⁻¹, s⁻¹","eli5":"how fast substrate sticks on and falls off"}],"eli5":"Picture a car wash. $V_{\\max}$ is the top speed when every bay is full. $K_m$ is how many cars must be waiting in line before the wash runs at half speed. A low $K_m$ wash gets busy even with a short line. But $K_m$ is not pure \"stickiness\": it mixes how well cars pull in ($k_1$, $k_{-1}$) with how fast they get washed ($k_{\\text{cat}}$). That's why we call it **apparent affinity**.","points":["$K_m$ = $[S]$ at $\\tfrac{1}{2}V_{\\max}$. Low $K_m$ → half-speed reached at low $[S]$ → higher apparent affinity.","When $k_{\\text{cat}} \\ll k_{-1}$, $K_m \\approx K_d$ and it really does measure binding.","Efficiency is $k_{\\text{cat}}/K_m$: with the same $k_{\\text{cat}}$, a lower $K_m$ means a more efficient enzyme.","$V_{\\max}$ rises if you add more enzyme; $K_m$ does not change with enzyme amount (it is a property of the enzyme).","$[S] \\gg K_m$ → $v_0 \\approx V_{\\max}$ (zero order in $S$). $[S] \\ll K_m$ → $v_0$ rises in proportion to $[S]$ (first order).","Enzymes lower activation energy. They never change $\\Delta G$, $K_{\\text{eq}}$, or which direction is favored."],"traps":["\"$K_m$ = affinity\" is only a shortcut. A mutation that speeds up $k_{\\text{cat}}$ raises $K_m$ even if binding is unchanged.","Low $K_m$ by itself does not make an enzyme fast; $V_{\\max}$ and $k_{\\text{cat}}$ measure speed."],"mnemonics":["**$K_m$ = \"Kinda Medium\"**: the substrate level for medium (half) speed.","**$k_{\\text{cat}}$ = a cat eating mice**: how many substrates one enzyme \"eats\" per second.","Efficiency = **\"fast cat, low Km wins.\"**"],"fig":"bbfl01-mm.svg","figcap":"Michaelis-Menten (MM) graph: the curve levels off at Vmax; Km is the [S] where the rate is Vmax/2."},{"code":"BBFL-02","cat":"BIO","title":"Reading the Michaelis-Menten (MM) graph vs the Lineweaver-Burk (LB) graph","locked":true},{"code":"BBFL-03","cat":"BIO","title":"Enzyme inhibitors: what happens to Km and Vmax","hook":"One table, memorized cold, answers every inhibitor question.","formulas":[{"tex":"\\alpha = 1 + \\dfrac{[I]}{K_i}","say":"how strongly the inhibitor acts"},{"tex":"\\text{Competitive: } K_m^{\\text{app}} = \\alpha K_m,\\quad V_{\\max} \\text{ unchanged}","say":""},{"tex":"\\text{Noncompetitive: } V_{\\max}^{\\text{app}} = \\dfrac{V_{\\max}}{\\alpha},\\quad K_m \\text{ unchanged}","say":""},{"tex":"\\text{Uncompetitive: } K_m^{\\text{app}} = \\dfrac{K_m}{\\alpha'},\\quad V_{\\max}^{\\text{app}} = \\dfrac{V_{\\max}}{\\alpha'}","say":""}],"vars":[{"tex":"[I]","name":"inhibitor concentration","unit":"M","eli5":""},{"tex":"K_i","name":"inhibitor dissociation constant","unit":"M","eli5":"low $K_i$ = inhibitor binds tightly"},{"tex":"K_m^{\\text{app}}","name":"apparent Km","unit":"M","eli5":"the Km you measure with inhibitor present"}],"eli5":"**Competitive**: a kid sitting in your chair (active site). Bring enough friends (substrate) and you win the chair back, so $V_{\\max}$ is still reachable but you need more substrate ($K_m$ up). **Noncompetitive**: someone jams the machine from the side; more substrate can't fix it ($V_{\\max}$ down). **Uncompetitive**: the inhibitor only grabs enzymes already holding substrate and locks them, so both numbers drop.","points":[{"t":"Competitive (binds free enzyme at the active site):","sub":["$K_m$ ↑, $V_{\\max}$ same. LB lines cross **on the y-axis**. Overcome by adding more substrate."]},{"t":"Noncompetitive (binds E and ES equally, at another site):","sub":["$K_m$ same, $V_{\\max}$ ↓. LB lines cross **on the x-axis**."]},{"t":"Uncompetitive (binds only the ES complex):","sub":["$K_m$ ↓ **and** $V_{\\max}$ ↓ by the same factor. LB lines are **parallel**."]},{"t":"Mixed (binds E and ES, but not equally):","sub":["$V_{\\max}$ ↓; $K_m$ ↑ if it prefers E, ↓ if it prefers ES. LB lines cross left of the y-axis, off the x-axis."]},"Irreversible inhibitors (like aspirin on COX) act like removing enzyme: $V_{\\max}$ ↓."],"traps":["Only **competitive** inhibition can be overcome by flooding with substrate.","Uncompetitive inhibition **lowers** $K_m$ (apparent affinity goes up), even though the enzyme is inhibited."],"mnemonics":["**Competitive → Crosses at the Y-axis** (Km goes up, \"compete\" for the seat).","**Noncompetitive → No change in Km**, crosses at the x-axis.","**UNcompetitive → UNder for both** (Km and Vmax both drop), lines parallel like the two lines in \"U\"."],"fig":"bbfl03-inhibitors.svg","figcap":"Lineweaver-Burk patterns: competitive (same y-intercept), noncompetitive (same x-intercept), uncompetitive (parallel lines)."},{"code":"BBFL-04","cat":"BIO","title":"Isoelectric point (pI) and amino acid charge","hook":"Average the right two pKas and you have the pI.","formulas":[{"tex":"\\text{pI} = \\dfrac{\\text{p}K_{a1} + \\text{p}K_{a2}}{2}","say":"neutral side chain: average the two backbone pKas"},{"tex":"\\text{pI} = \\dfrac{\\text{p}K_{a,\\text{COOH}} + \\text{p}K_{a,\\text{side}}}{2}","say":"acidic side chain (Asp, Glu)"},{"tex":"\\text{pI} = \\dfrac{\\text{p}K_{a,\\text{NH}_3^+} + \\text{p}K_{a,\\text{side}}}{2}","say":"basic side chain (Lys, Arg, His)"}],"vars":[{"tex":"\\text{pI}","name":"isoelectric point","unit":"pH units","eli5":"the pH where net charge = 0"},{"tex":"\\text{p}K_{a1}","name":"α-carboxyl pKa","unit":"≈ 2","eli5":""},{"tex":"\\text{p}K_{a2}","name":"α-amino pKa","unit":"≈ 9–9.5","eli5":""},{"tex":"\\text{p}K_{a,\\text{side}}","name":"side-chain pKa","unit":"","eli5":"Asp 3.9, Glu 4.1, His 6.0, Cys 8.3, Tyr 10.1, Lys 10.5, Arg 12.5"}],"eli5":"Each group holds onto its H⁺ when the pH is below its p$K_a$ and lets go above it. The pI is the pH where the + and − charges cancel. Rule of thumb: average the two p$K_a$ values on either side of the neutral (zwitterion) form.","points":["Neutral amino acids: pI ≈ (2 + 9.5)/2 ≈ 5.5–6.","Acidic: Asp pI ≈ (2 + 3.9)/2 ≈ 3. Basic: Lys ≈ (9 + 10.5)/2 ≈ 9.8, Arg ≈ 10.8, His ≈ 7.6.","pH **below** pI → net **positive**. pH **above** pI → net **negative**.","At physiological pH 7.4: Asp, Glu are −; Lys, Arg are +; His is mostly neutral (p$K_a$ 6).","Isoelectric focusing: a protein moves through a pH gradient and stops where pH = its pI."],"traps":["For acidic amino acids, average the two **low** pKas; for basic ones, average the two **high** pKas. Never average all three."],"mnemonics":["**\"pH Below pI → Bonus protons → Positive.\"**","**\"Protons stay on when pH is below pKa.\"**"],"fig":"bbfl04-gly.svg","figcap":"Glycine titration curve: plateaus at pKa1 ≈ 2.3 and pKa2 ≈ 9.6; pI = (2.3 + 9.6)/2 ≈ 6.0 at the first equivalence point."},{"code":"BBFL-06","cat":"BIO","title":"The 20 amino acids by group","hook":"Know each group, its one-letter codes, and the oddballs (Gly, Pro, Cys, His).","formulas":[],"vars":[],"eli5":"Sort amino acids by how their side chain feels about water. Nonpolar ones are greasy and hide inside proteins. Polar ones like water. Acidic ones carry a − charge and basic ones carry a + charge at body pH.","points":[{"t":"Nonpolar (hydrophobic): G A V L I M P F W","sub":["Glycine, Alanine, Valine, Leucine, Isoleucine, Methionine, Proline, Phenylalanine, Tryptophan."]},{"t":"Polar, uncharged: S T C N Q Y","sub":["Serine, Threonine, Cysteine, Asparagine, Glutamine, Tyrosine."]},{"t":"Acidic (negative at pH 7.4): D E","sub":["Aspartate (Asp, D), Glutamate (Glu, E)."]},{"t":"Basic (positive at pH 7.4): K R H","sub":["Lysine (K), Arginine (R), Histidine (H; p$K_a$ 6, so only partly charged)."]},{"t":"Aromatic: F Y W","sub":["Absorb UV at 280 nm (mostly Trp and Tyr), which is how we measure protein concentration."]},{"t":"Oddballs:","sub":["**Glycine**: side chain is just H → achiral, very flexible.","**Proline**: ring locks the backbone → breaks α-helices, common in turns.","**Cysteine**: SH group forms disulfide bridges (–S–S–). Cys and Met contain sulfur.","**Histidine**: p$K_a$ near 7 → buffers and acts as an acid/base in active sites.","Ser, Thr, Tyr have OH groups → sites of phosphorylation by kinases."]},"All amino acids in human proteins are L. All chiral L-amino acids are (S) except Cysteine, which is (R).","One-letter codes that don't match: **F** = Phe (\"Fenylalanine\"), **W** = Trp (\"tWyptophan\", double ring), **Y** = Tyr, **R** = Arg (\"aRginine\"), **K** = Lys (K is next to L), **D** = Asp (\"asparDic\"), **E** = Glu (\"glutEmate\"), **N** = Asn (\"asparagiNe\"), **Q** = Gln (\"Q-tamine\")."],"traps":["Tyrosine is aromatic **and** polar (its OH). Tryptophan is aromatic and usually grouped with nonpolar.","Asparagine (N) and glutamine (Q) are the **amides** of aspartate and glutamate: polar but **not** charged."],"mnemonics":["Nonpolar **G A V L I M P F W**: **\"Grumpy Aliens Visit Lonely Islands; Many Penguins Fly Westward.\"**","Polar **S T C N Q Y**: **\"STaCy Never Quits Yoga.\"**","Acidic **D E**: **\"DEad negative\"**: D and E carry the − charge.","Basic **K R H**: **\"Kinda Really Happy\"**: happy = positive.","Aromatic **F Y W**: **\"For Your Whiff\"** (aromatic = smells).","Essential amino acids: **\"PVT TIM HaLL\"**: Phe, Val, Thr, Trp, Ile, Met, His, Leu, Lys (Arg only in growth)."],"fig":"bbfl06-aa.svg","figcap":"Side chains grouped by property. Structures drawn from verified SMILES at pH 7.4."},{"code":"BBFL-07","cat":"BIO","title":"Hemoglobin and the O₂ dissociation curve","hook":"Sigmoid vs hyperbola, and what shifts the curve right or left.","formulas":[],"vars":[{"tex":"P_{50}","name":"O₂ partial pressure at 50% saturation","unit":"mmHg","eli5":"Hb ≈ 26 mmHg, myoglobin ≈ 2–3 mmHg"},{"tex":"\\text{Hb},\\ \\text{Mb}","name":"hemoglobin, myoglobin","unit":"","eli5":"Hb: 4 subunits, carries O₂ in blood; Mb: 1 subunit, stores O₂ in muscle"}],"eli5":"Hemoglobin is a delivery truck with four seats. Once one O₂ sits down, the others hop on more easily (cooperativity), which makes the S-shaped curve. In hard-working tissue (hot, acidic, lots of CO₂) the truck lets go of O₂ more easily: that's a right shift. Myoglobin is a storage locker: it grabs O₂ tightly and only lets go when muscle is desperate.","points":["Hb: sigmoidal curve (cooperative binding). Mb: hyperbolic curve (one site, no cooperativity), higher affinity.","**Right shift** = lower affinity = more O₂ unloaded to tissues; $P_{50}$ goes **up**.","Right shift causes: ↑CO₂, ↑H⁺ (↓pH), ↑2,3-BPG, exercise, ↑temperature.","**Left shift** = higher affinity: fetal hemoglobin (HbF), carbon monoxide, ↓temperature, ↑pH, ↓2,3-BPG.","Bohr effect: H⁺ and CO₂ lower Hb's O₂ affinity.","CO binds Hb about 200–250× more tightly than O₂, blocking seats **and** shifting the curve left."],"traps":["Fetal Hb binds 2,3-BPG **poorly**, so its curve is shifted **left** (it pulls O₂ from mom).","Right shift means \"lets go more easily,\" not \"carries more.\""],"mnemonics":["**\"CADET, face Right!\"**: CO₂, Acid, 2,3-DPG (BPG), Exercise, Temperature.","**\"Fetal = Firm grip = Left.\"**"],"fig":"bbfl07-hb.svg","figcap":"Hemoglobin (sigmoid) and myoglobin (hyperbola). A right shift raises P50 and unloads more O₂."},{"code":"BBFL-11","cat":"BIO","title":"Metabolism: the rate-limiting enzymes","locked":true},{"code":"BBFL-05","cat":"BIO","title":"Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium","locked":true},{"code":"BBFL-08","cat":"BIO","title":"DNA replication and the central dogma","locked":true},{"code":"BBFL-09","cat":"BIO","title":"Mitosis vs meiosis","locked":true},{"code":"BBFL-10","cat":"BIO","title":"Immunology: innate vs adaptive","locked":true},{"code":"OChem-01","cat":"ORG","title":"Acidity: what makes an acid strong (ARIO)","locked":true},{"code":"OChem-02","cat":"ORG","title":"SN1 vs SN2","locked":true},{"code":"OChem-03","cat":"ORG","title":"E1 vs E2: Zaitsev vs Hofmann","locked":true},{"code":"OChem-04","cat":"ORG","title":"Stereochemistry: R/S and D/L","locked":true},{"code":"OChem-05","cat":"ORG","title":"Spectroscopy: IR and ¹H NMR","locked":true},{"code":"OChem-06","cat":"ORG","title":"Carbonyls: reactivity and redox","locked":true},{"code":"Lab-01","cat":"LAB","title":"Blotting: Southern, Northern, Western","locked":true},{"code":"Lab-02","cat":"LAB","title":"Gel electrophoresis and SDS-PAGE","hook":"DNA runs to red (+), and small things run farthest.","formulas":[],"vars":[],"eli5":"A gel is a jungle gym. An electric field pulls charged molecules through it. Small molecules slip through quickly and travel far; big ones get tangled and stay near the start.","points":["DNA and RNA are negative (phosphate backbone) → migrate toward the **positive anode**.","Smaller fragments travel **farther**. Compare to a ladder of known sizes.","Agarose gels for DNA; polyacrylamide gels (PAGE) for proteins.","SDS-PAGE: SDS unfolds proteins and coats them with uniform negative charge → separates by **mass only**.","Adding β-mercaptoethanol or DTT breaks disulfide bonds → subunits run separately.","Native PAGE: protein stays folded → separates by size, shape, and charge; activity is kept.","Isoelectric focusing: protein stops where pH = pI (net charge 0)."],"traps":["In electrophoresis (an electrolytic setup) the anode is **positive**. Oxidation is still at the anode, reduction still at the cathode."],"mnemonics":["**\"Run to red\"**: DNA runs to the red (+) lead.","**\"Small = Swift.\"**"],"fig":"lab02-gel.svg","figcap":"Agarose gel: wells at the negative end; DNA runs toward +; small fragments travel farthest."},{"code":"Lab-03","cat":"LAB","title":"Chromatography: TLC, column and HPLC","locked":true},{"code":"Lab-04","cat":"LAB","title":"PCR: polymerase chain reaction","locked":true},{"code":"Lab-05","cat":"LAB","title":"Spectrophotometry: the Beer-Lambert law","locked":true}]};
